<ArticleLayout title={page.title} hreflang={hreflang} description={page.description} canonical={page.canonical} author={page.author} date={page.date} ogImage="/images/bulk-french-fries.jpg" bodyHtml={`<h2><strong>The french fries HS Code 20041000</strong></h2><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5" dir="auto"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words light"><p>French fries are a significant product in the global food trade, involving exports, imports, tariffs, market competition, and various trade regulations. This article provides a detailed analysis of the international trade of French fries. we will look at the trade data, key export/import countries, regulations, and price trends to help food traders and importers make informed decisions.</p><hr/><h2><strong>1. Why Are People Searching for “French Fries HS Code”?</strong></h2><p>If you are likely trying to:<br/><img alt="✅" src="https://s.w.org/images/core/emoji/15.0.3/svg/2705.svg"/> <strong>Import French fries</strong> and need to understand customs classifications, tariffs, and clearance procedures.<br/><img alt="✅" src="https://s.w.org/images/core/emoji/15.0.3/svg/2705.svg"/> <strong>Export French fries</strong> and want insights into target market regulations and competitors.<br/><img alt="✅" src="https://s.w.org/images/core/emoji/15.0.3/svg/2705.svg"/> <strong>Monitor market trends</strong> to analyze global supply chains and pricing before making purchasing or investment decisions.</p><p>It is important to mesmerize the French Fries HS Code <strong>20041000</strong><br/>Bad example: A Chinese food distributor planned to import <strong>French fries from Belgium</strong>, using HS Code <strong>20041000</strong> for frozen potato products. However, the company was unaware of <strong>China’s CIQ food safety certification</strong> requirements, leading to customs clearance delays and disruptions in its supply chain.</p><hr/><h2><strong>2. Top Exporters of French Fries (Latest 2024 Data)</strong></h2><p>According to <strong>2023 data from the International Trade Centre (ITC) and World’s Top Exports</strong>, the leading exporters of French fries are:</p><div class="overflow-x-auto contain-inline-size"><table border="1" cellpadding="2" cellspacing="0"><thead><tr><th><strong>Rank</strong></th><th><strong>Exporting Country</strong></th><th><strong>2023 Export Value (Billion USD)</strong></th><th><strong>Global Market Share</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td><strong>Belgium</strong> <img alt="🇧🇪" src="https://s.w.org/images/core/emoji/15.0.3/svg/1f1e7-1f1ea.svg"/></td><td><strong>5.78</strong></td><td>24.2%</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td><strong>Netherlands</strong> <img alt="🇳🇱" src="https://s.w.org/images/core/emoji/15.0.3/svg/1f1f3-1f1f1.svg"/></td><td><strong>4.35</strong></td><td>18.2%</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td><strong>Canada</strong> <img alt="🇨🇦" src="https://s.w.org/images/core/emoji/15.0.3/svg/1f1e8-1f1e6.svg"/></td><td><strong>2.31</strong></td><td>9.7%</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td><strong>United States</strong> <img alt="🇺🇸" src="https://s.w.org/images/core/emoji/15.0.3/svg/1f1fa-1f1f8.svg"/></td><td><strong>2.24</strong></td><td>9.4%</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td><strong>Argentina</strong> <img alt="🇦🇷" src="https://s.w.org/images/core/emoji/15.0.3/svg/1f1e6-1f1f7.svg"/></td><td><strong>1.32</strong></td><td>5.5%</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3><strong>Why Are Belgium and the Netherlands the Largest Exporters?</strong></h3><p><img alt="🔹" src="https://s.w.org/images/core/emoji/15.0.3/svg/1f539.svg"/> <strong>Superior Potato Cultivation</strong> – These countries have ideal conditions for high-starch potatoes, perfect for French fries.<br/><img alt="🔹" src="https://s.w.org/images/core/emoji/15.0.3/svg/1f539.svg"/> <strong>Advanced Processing Technology</strong> – Cutting-edge frozen food processing facilities ensure high quality.<br/><img alt="🔹" src="https://s.w.org/images/core/emoji/15.0.3/svg/1f539.svg"/> <strong>Strong Logistics &amp; Supply Chains</strong> – Efficient ports make global exports cost-effective.</p><p><strong>Case Study:</strong><br/>In 2023, <strong>Belgian company Agristo</strong> exported over <strong>1 million tons of frozen French fries</strong>, with <strong>50% destined for Asian markets</strong> (China, Japan, South Korea). Strategic partnerships with fast-food chains like McDonald’s and Burger King boosted their global reach.</p><hr/><h2><strong>3. Top Importers of French Fries (Latest 2024 Data)</strong></h2><p>The demand for frozen French fries is increasing worldwide, particularly in <strong>Asia and the Middle East</strong>. The largest importers of French fries in 2023 were:</p><div class="overflow-x-auto contain-inline-size"><table border="1" cellpadding="2" cellspacing="0"><thead><tr><th><strong>Rank</strong></th><th><strong>Importing Country</strong></th><th><strong>2023 Import Value (Billion USD)</strong></th><th><strong>Global Market Share</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td><strong>United States</strong> <img alt="🇺🇸" src="https://s.w.org/images/core/emoji/15.0.3/svg/1f1fa-1f1f8.svg"/></td><td><strong>3.04</strong></td><td>14.5%</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td><strong>Germany</strong> <img alt="🇩🇪" src="https://s.w.org/images/core/emoji/15.0.3/svg/1f1e9-1f1ea.svg"/></td><td><strong>2.16</strong></td><td>10.3%</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td><strong>Japan</strong> <img alt="🇯🇵" src="https://s.w.org/images/core/emoji/15.0.3/svg/1f1ef-1f1f5.svg"/></td><td><strong>1.98</strong></td><td>9.5%</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td><strong>China</strong> <img alt="🇨🇳" src="https://s.w.org/images/core/emoji/15.0.3/svg/1f1e8-1f1f3.svg"/></td><td><strong>1.87</strong></td><td>8.9%</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td><strong>United Kingdom</strong> <img alt="🇬🇧" src="https://s.w.org/images/core/emoji/15.0.3/svg/1f1ec-1f1e7.svg"/></td><td><strong>1.62</strong></td><td>7.7%</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3><strong>Why Are These Countries Major Importers?</strong></h3><p><img alt="✅" src="https://s.w.org/images/core/emoji/15.0.3/svg/2705.svg"/> <strong>United States</strong> – Although the US produces its own French fries, it imports large quantities from <strong>Canada and the Netherlands</strong> to meet demand.<br/><img alt="✅" src="https://s.w.org/images/core/emoji/15.0.3/svg/2705.svg"/> <strong>Germany, Japan, and the UK</strong> – Strong fast-food industries drive consistent demand.<br/><img alt="✅" src="https://s.w.org/images/core/emoji/15.0.3/svg/2705.svg"/> <strong>China</strong> – Rapid growth in quick-service restaurants (KFC, Pizza Hut, and local chains) fuels increased imports.</p><p><strong>Case Study:</strong><br/>In 2023, <strong>China imported over 1 million tons of French fries</strong>, <strong>with more than 50% sourced from Belgium and the Netherlands</strong>. As frozen food consumption rises in second-tier and third-tier cities, China’s demand for imported French fries is expected to keep growing.</p><hr/><h2><strong>4. Trade Details: Tariffs, Regulations, and Logistics</strong></h2><h3><strong>Import Tariffs for French Fries (2024 Rates)</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>China</strong>: 10% tariff + 9% VAT</li><li><strong>Japan</strong>: 8.5% tariff</li><li><strong>India</strong>: 30% tariff (to protect domestic agriculture)</li><li><strong>European Union</strong>: Zero tariffs within the EU (Belgium/Netherlands have free trade)</li><li><strong>United States</strong>: 0% tariff from <strong>Canada</strong>, 8% from other countries</li></ul><h3><strong>Food Safety &amp; Labeling Requirements</strong></h3><p><img alt="📌" src="https://s.w.org/images/core/emoji/15.0.3/svg/1f4cc.svg"/> <strong>China</strong> – Requires CIQ (China Inspection and Quarantine) approval and compliance with <strong>GB 2760 food additive standards</strong>.<br/><img alt="📌" src="https://s.w.org/images/core/emoji/15.0.3/svg/1f4cc.svg"/> <strong>European Union</strong> – Products must follow <strong>HACCP food safety standards</strong> and label allergens.<br/><img alt="📌" src="https://s.w.org/images/core/emoji/15.0.3/svg/1f4cc.svg"/> <strong>Middle East</strong> – Countries like Saudi Arabia require <strong>Halal certification</strong> for imported food.</p><h3><strong>Cold Chain Logistics Requirements</strong></h3><ul><li>French fries must be <strong>transported at -18°C</strong> to maintain quality and prevent oxidation.</li><li><strong>Port storage regulations</strong>: Import hubs like <strong>Shanghai Port (China) or Jebel Ali Port (Dubai)</strong> require frozen goods to be stored in designated cold storage facilities to avoid clearance delays.</li></ul><hr/><h2><strong>5. Market Price Trends for French Fries (2024 Q1 Data)</strong></h2><p>French fries’ prices are influenced by <strong>weather conditions, logistics costs, and global supply chain fluctuations</strong>. As of early 2024, price trends were:</p><div class="overflow-x-auto contain-inline-size"><table border="1" cellpadding="2" cellspacing="0"><thead><tr><th><strong>Exporting Country</strong></th><th><strong>2023 Average Price (USD/ton)</strong></th><th><strong>2024 Q1 Price (USD/ton)</strong></th><th><strong>Year-over-Year Change</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Belgium</strong> <img alt="🇧🇪" src="https://s.w.org/images/core/emoji/15.0.3/svg/1f1e7-1f1ea.svg"/></td><td><strong>750</strong></td><td><strong>820</strong></td><td><img alt="🔺" src="https://s.w.org/images/core/emoji/15.0.3/svg/1f53a.svg"/>9.3%</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Netherlands</strong> <img alt="🇳🇱" src="https://s.w.org/images/core/emoji/15.0.3/svg/1f1f3-1f1f1.svg"/></td><td><strong>770</strong></td><td><strong>850</strong></td><td><img alt="🔺" src="https://s.w.org/images/core/emoji/15.0.3/svg/1f53a.svg"/>10.3%</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Canada</strong> <img alt="🇨🇦" src="https://s.w.org/images/core/emoji/15.0.3/svg/1f1e8-1f1e6.svg"/></td><td><strong>710</strong></td><td><strong>740</strong></td><td><img alt="🔺" src="https://s.w.org/images/core/emoji/15.0.3/svg/1f53a.svg"/>4.2%</td></tr><tr><td><strong>United States</strong> <img alt="🇺🇸" src="https://s.w.org/images/core/emoji/15.0.3/svg/1f1fa-1f1f8.svg"/></td><td><strong>730</strong></td><td><strong>765</strong></td><td><img alt="🔺" src="https://s.w.org/images/core/emoji/15.0.3/svg/1f53a.svg"/>4.8%</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3><strong>Why Are Prices Increasing?</strong></h3><p><img alt="📈" src="https://s.w.org/images/core/emoji/15.0.3/svg/1f4c8.svg"/> <strong>Higher Logistics Costs</strong> – Rising fuel prices impact transportation expenses.<br/><img alt="📈" src="https://s.w.org/images/core/emoji/15.0.3/svg/1f4c8.svg"/> <strong>Potato Yield Variability</strong> – Weather conditions affect supply; droughts in some regions cause shortages.<br/><img alt="📈" src="https://s.w.org/images/core/emoji/15.0.3/svg/1f4c8.svg"/> <strong>Rising Fast-Food Demand</strong> – Increased consumption in <strong>Asia and the Middle East</strong> keeps prices high.</p><hr/><h2><strong>Conclusion</strong></h2><p>French fries are a growing global trade commodity, with <strong>Belgium, the Netherlands, and the US</strong> dominating exports, while <strong>China, Japan, and Germany</strong> lead in imports. <strong>Understanding tariffs, food safety regulations, and logistics</strong> is crucial for businesses looking to enter this market. Keeping an eye on <strong>price trends and demand fluctuations</strong> helps optimize purchasing and export strategies.</p><p><img alt="🚀" src="https://s.w.org/images/core/emoji/15.0.3/svg/1f680.svg"/> <strong>If you’re a food trader looking to source high-quality French fries or expand into export markets, contact us for more market insights and business opportunities!</strong></p></div></div></div></div>`} />
